\section*{AP \({ }^{\text {® }}\) CALCULUS BC \\ 2008 SCORING GUIDELINES (Form B)}

\section*{Question 2}

For time \(t \geq 0\) hours, let \(r(t)=120\left(1-e^{-10 t^{2}}\right)\) represent the speed, in kilometers per hour, at which a car travels along a straight road. The number of liters of gasoline used by the car to travel \(x\) kilometers is modeled by \(g(x)=0.05 x\left(1-e^{-x / 2}\right)\).
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] How many kilometers does the car travel during the first 2 hours?
\item[(b)] Find the rate of change with respect to time of the number of liters of gasoline used by the car when \(t=2\) hours. Indicate units of measure.
\item[(c)] How many liters of gasoline have been used by the car when it reaches a speed of 80 kilometers per hour?
\item[(a)] \(\int_{0}^{2} r(t) d t=206.370\) kilometers
\item[(b)] \(\frac{d g}{d t}=\frac{d g}{d x} \cdot \frac{d x}{d t} ; \quad \frac{d x}{d t}=r(t)\)
\end{itemize}
\[
\begin{aligned}
\left.\frac{d g}{d t}\right|_{t=2} & =\left.\frac{d g}{d x}\right|_{x=206.370} \cdot r(2) \\
& =(0.050)(120)=6 \text { liters } / \text { hour }
\end{aligned}
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[] \(2:\left\{\begin{array}{l}1: \text { integral } \\ 1: \text { answer }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[3] : \(\left\{\begin{array}{l}2 \text { : uses chain rule } \\ 1 \text { : answer with units }\end{array}\right.\)
\item[(c)] Let \(T\) be the time at which the car's speed reaches 80 kilometers per hour.
Then, \(r(T)=80\) or \(T=0.331453\) hours.
At time \(T\), the car has gone
\[
x(T)=\int_{0}^{T} r(t) d t=10.794097 \text { kilometers }
\]
and has consumed \(g(x(T))=0.537\) liters of gasoline.
\end{itemize}
